Lastly, similar to MiniMax-M1, MiniMax-M2 uses a =E2=80=9Cpartial=E2=80=9D =
instead of regular RoPE inside the attention modules to encode positional i=
nformation. Similar to regular RoPE, the rotations are applied to the queri=
es and keys after applying QK-Norm.</p><p><span>Partial RoPE here means onl=
y the first </span><code>rotary_dim</code><span> channels of each head get =
rotary position encodings, and the remaining </span><code>head_dim - rotary=
_dim</code><span> channels remain unchanged.</span></p><p><span>In the offi=
cial M1 </span><a href=3D"https://github.com/MiniMax-AI/MiniMax-01">README<=
/a><span> file, the developers mention</span></p><blockquote><p>Rotary Posi=
tion Embedding (RoPE) applied to half of the attention head dimension with =
a base frequency of 10,000,000</p></blockquote><p>We can picture it as foll=
ows:</p><pre><code>Full RoPE:     [r r r r r r r r]
Partial RoPE:  [r r r r =E2=80=94 =E2=80=94 =E2=80=94 =E2=80=94]</code></pr=
e><p>where in the conceptual illustration above, the =E2=80=9Cr=E2=80=9Ds s=
how rotated (position-encoded) dimensions, and the dashes are the untouched=
 dimensions.</p><p><span>What=E2=80=99s the point of this? In the </span><a=
 href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.08313">M1 paper</a><span>, the develope=
rs stated that</span></p><blockquote><p>=E2=80=A6implementing RoPE on half =
of the softmax attention dimensions enables length extrapolation without pe=
rformance degradation.</p></blockquote><p>My speculation is that this preve=
nts =E2=80=9Ctoo much=E2=80=9D rotation for long sequences, and particularl=
y those that are longer than the longest documents in the training dataset.=
 I.e., the rationale here could be that no rotation is better than a =E2=80=
=9Cbad=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Ctoo extreme=E2=80=9D rotation that the model ha=
sn=E2=80=99t seen before in training.</p><p></p><h1 class=3D"header-anchor-=
