) show a single next-token prediction.</span></figcaption></figure></div><p=
>A key difference from the standard uses of MTP (which I've drawn in figure=
 51.2 above) is that Nemotron 3 Super does not use it only during training.=
 </p><p>The Nemotron 3 Super explicitly uses MTP at inference time as well,=
 where the shared-weight MTP head acts as an internal draft model for nativ=
e speculative decoding. During generation, the model can then propose candi=
date continuations and then verify them with the main model. This reduces i=
nference latency without needing a separate external draft model.</p><p><sp=
an>Since this is not quite standard MTP, it is perhaps more accurate to des=
cribe Nemotron 3 Super as using shared-weight MTP for speculative decoding =
than to call it something like =E2=80=9CMTP-3=E2=80=9D like in other archit=
ectures (like Step 3.5 Flash, which I covered </span><a href=3D"https://mag=
azine.sebastianraschka.com/p/a-dream-of-spring-for-open-weight">here</a><sp=
an>).</span></p><p>The second main difference compared to Nano is that the =
Super architecture uses latent experts, meaning that the experts operate in=
 latent space (the inputs to the MoE layer are down-projected from 4096 to =
1024 dimensions, the experts are applied, and then the outputs are up-proje=
cted back from 1024 to 4096 dimensions.</p><div class=3D"captioned-image-co=
